{"assets":["/editorial-assets/f088307859c54a592db8abbb8a991e898200bc8109ca46baf450ac90661ad738.png","/editorial-assets/32294f4fcc7edbba7be0cfc2fdc07ee42b888ba54199d93be7cb4c0b8e020e1d.png","/editorial-assets/a41a37cfe292e04b43fac23ef0d90a007539a46387a59cd625a618c100fdeabb.png","/editorial-assets/e89cb1765a34051e8aa856ed8aa0a310fede2ed07f8ed838b66ef7102360f924.png","/editorial-assets/c0fdc5d22099edf88481a03c557443be3d1c3ae99670ff3e9f4da657f1d03e58.png","/editorial-assets/0cefacf9b386c225cee1a7efe10b8256d581ccdd59cfcd59318cd50695f6ad9f.png","/editorial-assets/106801f3b12df602735945484f39193492af1bd77d4e7a6067564d633d0da51e.png","/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png","/editorial-assets/940827e9e2d84837a1fa79b7139b957e7290b607a6b673a3dddd7e6b0151585a.png","/editorial-assets/d6043475de6abb038990c9d20ab67e0761b40a3e0c7b1cee0bb1f0b4165e6821.png","/editorial-assets/2ddc97f80ce4f089578ce5db5cd354e6919b0f7ea1b2a3020628a48af1499647.png","/editorial-assets/0f5710b61e253aed60c8c06f8650cfdbdfe3c243130312d812ab2bcee15ad0db.png"],"contestId":"309","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/308/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem A. Morning run"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We were asked to find the expected value of meetings between the runners. How to do that? As the first step, expected value is lineal, so we can split the initial problems into the different ones: find the expected value of meetings between the fixed pair of runners. We will solve these problems. To do that we need to make some observations:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the distance between the two runners and they run face to face for infinite amount of time (probability of that obviously equals to 0.5·0.5 = 0.25). Then the first meeting will happen at time "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f088307859c54a592db8abbb8a991e898200bc8109ca46baf450ac90661ad738.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", the next one — "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/32294f4fcc7edbba7be0cfc2fdc07ee42b888ba54199d93be7cb4c0b8e020e1d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", the next — "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/a41a37cfe292e04b43fac23ef0d90a007539a46387a59cd625a618c100fdeabb.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and so on."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us assume that every run ran for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" time units. Then if two runners meet — they meet exactly two times. The probability of the meeting equals to 0.5, because in two cases they run in the same direction and in two cases in the different ones."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will build our solution based on these two observations. As the first step let us represent "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where 0 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then each runner will run "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" full laps. What does that mean? Because we have "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e89cb1765a34051e8aa856ed8aa0a310fede2ed07f8ed838b66ef7102360f924.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" pairs of runners, then in those "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" laps each pair will have 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" meetings with probability equals to 0.5. So, we need to add "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c0fdc5d22099edf88481a03c557443be3d1c3ae99670ff3e9f4da657f1d03e58.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we need to take into account "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" seconds of running. Let us assume that the distance between two runners is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and they run towards each other. Then they will meet if "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0cefacf9b386c225cee1a7efe10b8256d581ccdd59cfcd59318cd50695f6ad9f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", or "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". They will meet once more if "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/106801f3b12df602735945484f39193492af1bd77d4e7a6067564d633d0da51e.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", ir "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". They cannot meet more than twice, because "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us fix one of the runners, then using binary search we can find all other runners at distance no more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from the fixed one. Let us choose "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and then the number of runners at the distance no more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" stands for the number of runners which meet with the fixed one at least once. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we will find the number of runners which meet with the fixed one exactly two times. Multiplying these numbers by 0.25 — probability of the meeting, and add it to the answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The complexity of this solution is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can reduce it using two pointers method."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/308/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem B. Context Advertising"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We were asked to find the maximal number of words we can fit into the block of size "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" × "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's first solve such problem: what is the maximal number of consecutive words which can fit in a row of lenth "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", if the first word has index "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can solve it using binary search or moving the pointer. Now let us build a graph, where vertices are the words and there is a directional edge between "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", if words from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 fit in one row of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", but words from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" don't. The weight of the edge is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The we have the following problem: we need to find the path of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", which has the maximal weight. Easy to solve it with complexity "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" saving weights of all the paths with lengthes equal to the powers of two, or in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time using dfs."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The other problems competitors faced — that we were asked to print the whole text, not only the length."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/308/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem C. Memory for Arrays"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We were asked to find the maximal number of arrays we can fit into the memory. A small observation first, let the answer be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then one of the optimal solutions fits the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" smallest arrays into the memory. We can assume that we have arrays of size 1 and we want to arrange the memory for the maximal arrays as possible. Then if we have parts of memory of odd size, if we fit array of size 1 at this part we will obtain part of even size. From other hand, if we put arrays of bigger size we will not change the parity and if we don't fill it with arrays of size one and initially it's of odd size then in the end we will have at least one empty cell. So it's reasonable to put the arrays of size one into the memory of odd size. Let's do it until we can do it. We have three possible situations:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We don't have memory parts of odd size anymore."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We don't have arrays of size 1 anymore."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We don't have neither arrays of size 1 neither memory parts of size 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us start from the first case. Suppose that there are some arrays of size 1 left, but there are no memory parts of odd size. Easy to see then in such case we need to group arrays of size 1 in pairs and then consider them as the same array. So we can divide every number by two and reduce the problem to the initial one."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the second case if we divide every number by two we will obtain the same problem (and that cannot increase the answer)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The third case is similar to the second one."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"When implementing this we need to remember that first we have to fill the memory with arrays which are build from the maximal numbers of initial arrays."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The complexity of the given algorithm is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/308/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem D. Tennis Rackets"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We were asked to find the number of obtuse triangles which satisfy the problem statement. Author's solution has complexity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), but it has some optimizations, so it easily works under the TL."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Every triangle has only one obtuse angle. Due to symmetry reasons we can fix one of the sides and assume that obtuse angle is tangent to this side. Then we only need to find the number of such triangles and multiple the answer by 3."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Every side is also symmetric, so we can consider only one half of it and then multiple the answer by 2."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us assume that vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of the triangle has coordinates (0,0). Vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (0,"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/940827e9e2d84837a1fa79b7139b957e7290b607a6b673a3dddd7e6b0151585a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":") and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(2,0). Then we can find the coordinates of every single point at each of the sides and apply cosine theorem. We obtain the inequality which guarantee us that the triangle is obtuse. It can be written in many ways, on of them is following: If 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" — indices of points which are fixed at each of the sides, then triangle is obtuse iff: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) + 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ij"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") < 0. We can see that "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d6043475de6abb038990c9d20ab67e0761b40a3e0c7b1cee0bb1f0b4165e6821.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" monotonically increases, so we can use moving pointer method applied to variable "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then just go over all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/2ddc97f80ce4f089578ce5db5cd354e6919b0f7ea1b2a3020628a48af1499647.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 till upper bound for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is less than or equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 and just sum the results."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We should mention that all of the operations have to be done in int type, it significantly increases the speed."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/308/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem E. Sheep"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Author's supposed greedy algorithm as a solution for this problem. Let us follow this algorithm. Let us create to label for every interval "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Position"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MaximalPosition"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Position"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" — stands for position of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the required permutation."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MaximalPosition"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" — stands for maximal possible position of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the particular moment of the algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also let's consider "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"count"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as a counter with initial value of 0 and set of unprocessed vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The algorithm is following."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Using binary search we find the maximal possible distance between the farthest sheep. And then check whether exists the permutation with maximal distance no more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"K"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Sort all the intervals in the increasing order of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Position"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0, 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MaximalPosition"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"current"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"count"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Do "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"count"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"